<Layout title="For Parents | Kin & Campus">
<div class="w ph"><div class="eyebrow">For parents</div><h1>They're far from home. We are local.</h1><p class="lead">A board-certified pediatrician based right in Blacksburg. Direct physician access, same-day appointments in town, and seamless coordination with the doctor who has known them since kindergarten.</p></div>
<div class="w"><img class="photo" src={href("photos/arch-fall.jpg")} width="678" height="452" alt="A stone arch on campus under fall trees">
<div class="eyebrow">Why parents look for this</div>
<h2>Many students already have a plan. It is hard to run from 250 miles away.</h2>
<p class="lead">The default after hours is often urgent care or the emergency room. A local pediatrician who already knows their baseline is the calmer logistics.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>The plan they already have</h3><p>In the Fall 2024 ACHA-NCHA III national sample, 16% of students reported ever being diagnosed with asthma. Forty-four percent reported at least one listed mental-health diagnosis (56% reported none of those listed). More than a third were diagnosed with a cold, virus, or other respiratory illness in the past year. Those plans do not pause at move-in.</p><p class="cite">ACHA-NCHA III, Fall 2024 reference group, 33,763 students at 48 U.S. institutions. <a href="https://www.acha.org/wp-content/uploads/NCHA-IIIb_FALL_2024_REFERENCE_GROUP_EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.pdf">Executive summary (PDF)</a>.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Care away from home</h3><p>Most students stay on a parent or guardian's insurance (58% in that same ACHA sample). After hours, illness does not keep office hours—and ACHA’s college-health framework treats after-hours access as something every campus program has to plan for, through on-call clinicians, contracted advice lines, or a local hospital partnership. The hometown pediatrician is not down the street.</p><p class="cite">Insurance mix: ACHA-NCHA III, Fall 2024. After-hours guidance: <a href="https://www.acha.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/ACHA_Framework_for_a_Comprehensive_College_Health_Program.pdf">ACHA Framework for a Comprehensive College Health Program</a> (2024).</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>The expensive default</h3><p>Among people with large-employer private insurance, an emergency department visit averaged $646 out of pocket. Allowed amounts are higher: UnitedHealthcare's 2025 network medians are about $2,750 for an ER visit versus about $195 at urgent care. A same-day visit with a doctor in Blacksburg who already knows them is the better first call—unless it is a true emergency, in which case call 911.</p><p class="cite">Out-of-pocket: <a href="https://www.kff.org/health-costs/emergency-department-visits-exceed-affordability-threshold-for-many-consumers-with-private-insurance/">KFF / Peterson-Kaiser Health System Tracker</a>, 2019 large-employer claims (published 2022). Allowed amounts: <a href="https://www.uhc.com/member-resources/where-to-go-for-medical-care/care-options-and-costs">UnitedHealthcare, 2025 network medians</a>.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:56px">What we hold while they're here</div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><div class="num">1</div><h3>Continuous chronic care</h3><p>Your established treatment plan stays active and monitored. We manage ongoing care locally and keep their hometown doctor fully updated when anything shifts.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="num">2</div><h3>A trusted physician nearby</h3><p>Having a board-certified pediatrician just eight minutes from campus means they have reliable oversight right in the same town, easily accessible between classes.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="num">3</div><h3>Building adult health habits</h3><p>College is where young adults take ownership of their health. We guide them through navigating appointments, refills, and self-care so they are prepared for independence.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="num">4</div><h3>Collaborative communication</h3><p>Respecting your student's adult privacy while keeping you informed. We establish clear communication boundaries together, and their hometown doctor receives a clinical update the same day.</p></div></div></div>
<section><div class="w"><div class="eyebrow">Nearby, in practice</div><h2>Built for college health.</h2><div class="grid"><div class="card"><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-chat"/></svg><h3>Direct physician access</h3><p>A direct line to their doctor in Blacksburg—eliminating call centers, long waitlists, and nurse triage phone trees.</p></div><div class="card"><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Same-day availability</h3><p>A strictly limited patient panel guarantees that when acute illness hits, they can be seen same-day right here in town.</p></div><div class="card"><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-compass"/></svg><h3>Integrated hometown coordination</h3><p>Their hometown pediatrician is designated on day one, copied on clinical notes, and provided a comprehensive year-end summary every May.</p></div></div>
<div style="text-align:center;margin-top:48px"><h2>One unnecessary ER trip is a costly default.</h2><p class="lead" style="margin:16px auto 28px">College health panics rarely happen during convenient hours. A midnight fever, asthma flare, or allergic reaction often sends students straight to the emergency room. A doctor in town who already knows their baseline is the calmer first call.</p>
<p><a class="btn" href={href("join")}>Reserve a spot</a> &nbsp; <a class="lnk" href={href("membership")}>Membership &rarr;</a></p></div></div></section>
</Layout>
